<p class="lead">
  iLovePDF and Smallpdf are the two names most people land on first when they search for a free PDF tool — both get hundreds of millions of visits a month, both are genuinely capable, and both work by uploading your file to their servers. keptlocal takes a different approach: everything runs in your browser instead. Here's an honest, feature-by-feature comparison to help you pick the right one for what you're actually doing.
</p>

<h2>The core architectural difference</h2>
<p>
  This is the one difference every other comparison in this article follows from. iLovePDF and Smallpdf process files server-side: your PDF uploads to their infrastructure, gets processed there, and the result downloads back to you. keptlocal processes files client-side: the same operations run in JavaScript inside your browser tab, and the file never leaves your device.
</p>
<p>
  Neither approach is universally "better" — server-side processing genuinely enables some things a browser can't do yet (heavy OCR, some PDF-to-Word conversions, extreme multi-pass compression). But for the large majority of everyday PDF tasks — merging, splitting, rotating, watermarking, compressing, converting to images, filling forms, adding page numbers — a browser can now do the same job, and the privacy and speed profile is meaningfully different when it does.
</p>

